Some writing arrives as a set of files. Some arrives as a whole project, made in another
writing application, with its chapters and its cast and its notes already sorted. Those are two
different jobs, and Skribisto does them in two different places.

This page is about the second one: bringing a project across from Manuskript or from Plume
Creator. If what you have is a folder of documents instead, see
[importing documents](:help-import-documents).

A project import always makes a **new** Skribisto project. It reads the one you point at and
never writes to it, so the original is exactly as you left it whatever happens, and you can try
the import twice if the first result is not what you wanted.

## Starting it

With a project open, use *Work ▸ Import from* and pick the application you are coming from.
From the launcher window, the same two rows are under *Create from…*, because a project import
needs nothing open to import into.

Either way you choose the project, then a destination folder and a file name for the Skribisto
`.skrib` it will create. Nothing is written until you press *Import*, and a large project shows
its progress with a *Cancel* button that leaves nothing behind.

## Coming from Manuskript

Manuskript stores a project in one of two ways, and the wizard takes either. In its usual mode
a project is a small `.msk` file **plus a folder of the same name beside it**, and you need
both: the `.msk` on its own is one byte long and only points at the folder. In its single-file
mode the whole project is inside the `.msk`. There is a *Choose file…* button and a *Choose
folder…* button, so you can point at whichever half you have in front of you.

Your manuscript comes across with its text, both of its summaries per row, its notes, its word
goals, and the "compile" tick that decides what belongs in the finished book. A row you had
switched off stays switched off, and so does everything inside it.

Your cast, your world entries and your plots become notes in a **Story bible** binder beside
the manuscript, each in its own group. A scene's point of view points at the character it names.
Plots keep their beats and their links to the characters they are about. Labels become tags,
keeping the colours you gave them, and your status list becomes Skribisto's workflow ladder
with your own names on the rungs, in your own language.

Where you mentioned a character, a place or a plot inside your prose, Manuskript stored a
marker. Those markers become the words they stood for, and the row gains a link to what it
named, so the text reads as prose and the connection is still there.

If your project kept a revision history, it comes too, and the *Versions* panel opens with your
own past in it. A long history is thinned the same way Skribisto thins its own.

### What does not come across, and why

A few things have no home here, and the import says so rather than dropping them quietly:

- **Per-row icons.** In Skribisto a row's icon comes from what the row is, so there is nothing
  to set.
- **The colour beside a character's name.** Skribisto colours tags rather than rows. The
  characters themselves all arrive.
- **The five nested summaries** of the whole book (situation, sentence, paragraph, page, full).
  Skribisto keeps one synopsis per row, so the longest of the five becomes the book's, and all
  five are kept together in a *Summary* note so none is lost.
- Details with no field of their own, such as the series and volume, land in a *Project
  information* note for the same reason.

## Coming from Manuskript: how the shape is worked out

Manuskript has folders and text files, and nothing in a project says which folders are parts
and which are chapters. Skribisto needs to know, so it reads the depth: if your folders are one
level deep they are chapters; if there are folders inside folders, the outer ones are parts and
the inner ones chapters. Everything is wrapped in one Book, which is what Skribisto's writing
plan, its analysis and its export scopes are built around.

It is a starting point, not a verdict. Any row can be retyped afterwards from the binder, and
nothing is lost by doing so. See [the writing model](:help-writing-model) for what each type
means.

## Coming from Plume Creator

Choose a `.plume` or `.plume_backup` file, of any Plume version. Books, acts, chapters and
scenes arrive with their text, their synopses and their notes; scene separators become scene
breaks in the prose above them; and the story bible's characters, places and items become notes
with their own group tags. Plume's eight revision stages arrive as your workflow ladder.

Items you had put in Plume's rubbish bin are **not** migrated. Empty the bin or restore what
you want to keep before importing.

## After the import

The import offers *Open now*, which opens the new project. If anything could not be carried
across exactly, a second notice appears with a *Details* button listing every one of them, in
plain language, with nothing hidden.

The new project is a single `.skrib` file. If you kept your Manuskript project in a folder so
you could put it in version control, use *Work ▸ Save as folder* afterwards: that is the same
arrangement, in Skribisto's own format. See
[backups and versions](:help-backups-and-versions) for the rest of what keeps your work safe.
